<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.4.2: Rewrite Squared Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02388</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.4.2: Rewrite Squared Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1251fb0c-a6fc-40a8-b224-541270dba0ec</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write each expression in standard form:</para>
    <para>\((4x+1)^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(16x^2+8x+1\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((5x−2)^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(25x^2−20x+4\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((\frac{1}{2}x+7)^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(\frac{1}{4}x^2+7x+49\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((3x+n)^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(9x^2+2(3)(n)x+n^2\) or \(9x^2+6nx+n^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((kx+m)^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(k^2x^2+2kmx+m^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Decide if each expression is a perfect square. If so, write an equivalent expression of the form \((kx+m)^2\). If not, suggest one change to turn it into a perfect square.</para>
    <para>\(4x^2+12x+9\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Yes, a perfect square. \((2x+3)^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(4x^2+8x+25\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>No, not a perfect square. Suggested changes include:</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent">
    <item> Changing the coefficient of the linear term to 20 gives \(4x^2+20x+25\), which is equivalent to \((2x+5)^2\). </item>
    <item> Changing the coefficient of the linear term to -20 gives \(4x^2−20x+25\), which is equivalent to \((2x−5)^2\). </item>
    <item> Changing the constant term to 4 gives \(4x^2+8x+4\), which is equivalent to \((2x+2)^2\). </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example><title>Video: Rewrite Squared Expressions</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about rewriting squared expressions.</para>
